<h2>What to send (and what not to)</h2>

<p>The biggest mistake in email marketing for small business is treating every email like a sales pitch. If every message you send is "Buy this! Book now! Limited time offer!" people will stop opening them. Fast.</p>

<p>Here is a mix that keeps your audience engaged without burning them out:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Welcome sequences.</strong> When someone joins your list, send them a short series of emails introducing your business, what you do, and how you can help. This is your chance to make a first impression and set expectations for what they will receive from you going forward.</li>
  <li><strong>Newsletters.</strong> Regular updates that provide value. Tips, insights, industry news, behind-the-scenes looks at your business. The goal is to be useful enough that people look forward to seeing your name in their inbox.</li>
  <li><strong>Promotions.</strong> Yes, you can sell. Just do not make it every email. A good rule of thumb is the 80/20 split. Eighty percent value, twenty percent sales. When you do promote something, make the offer clear, the deadline real, and the call to action obvious.</li>
  <li><strong>Re-engagement campaigns.</strong> Some subscribers will go quiet. After a few months of no opens, send a "We miss you" email with a reason to come back. If they still do not engage, remove them from your list. A smaller, active list outperforms a large, disengaged one every time.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How often should you send?</h2>

<p>This is the question every small business asks, and the answer is frustratingly simple. Consistency matters more than frequency.</p>

<p>Sending one email per week, every week, is better than sending five emails in one week and then going silent for a month. Your audience needs to know what to expect from you. If you commit to a fortnightly newsletter, stick to it. If you can manage weekly, even better. But do not overcommit and then disappear.</p>

<p>Start with a frequency you can maintain without burning out. You can always increase it later once you have a system in place. Watch your unsubscribe rate. If it spikes after a particular send, that is your audience telling you something. Listen to them.</p>

<h2>Automations that do the heavy lifting</h2>

<p>This is where <a href="/blog/marketing-automation-getting-started">email marketing gets powerful for small businesses</a>. Automations let you send the right message at the right time without lifting a finger after the initial setup.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Welcome series.</strong> Automatically introduce new subscribers to your business over three to five emails. This runs in the background forever, giving every new contact the same great first experience.</li>
  <li><strong>Abandoned cart or booking follow-ups.</strong> If someone starts a purchase or booking but does not finish, an automated email reminding them can recover a surprising percentage of lost revenue. These emails often have the highest conversion rates of anything you send.</li>
  <li><strong>Post-purchase follow-ups.</strong> After someone buys from you or completes an appointment, send a thank you, ask for a review, or suggest a related product or service. This is where repeat business is built.</li>
  <li><strong>Birthday and anniversary emails.</strong> Simple, personal, and effective. A birthday discount or a "one year since you joined us" message makes people feel remembered. That feeling builds loyalty.</li>
</ul>

<p>The beauty of automations is that you build them once and they keep working. A small business with five or six well-built automations can deliver a personalised experience that rivals companies ten times their size.</p>

<blockquote><p>The best email marketing does not feel like marketing. It feels like a business that remembers you and cares about being helpful.</p></blockquote>

