<h2>Measuring what matters</h2>

<p>You do not need a degree in analytics to understand your email performance. Focus on four numbers:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Open rate.</strong> The percentage of people who open your email. Anything above 20% is decent. Above 30% is strong. If you are consistently below 15%, your subject lines need work or your list quality is poor.</li>
  <li><strong>Click rate.</strong> The percentage who click a link inside your email. This tells you whether your content is compelling enough to drive action. A healthy click rate sits between 2% and 5% for most industries.</li>
  <li><strong>Unsubscribe rate.</strong> Some unsubscribes are normal and healthy. If your rate is under 0.5% per send, you are fine. If it starts climbing above 1%, something is wrong. Either the content is off, the frequency is too high, or you attracted the wrong subscribers in the first place.</li>
  <li><strong>Revenue per email.</strong> If you are selling products or services through email, track how much revenue each campaign generates. This is the number that matters most, because it connects your email efforts directly to your bottom line.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common mistakes that kill your results</h2>

<p>Most small businesses make the same handful of errors with email marketing. Avoid these and you are already ahead of most of your competitors:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Sending without a plan.</strong> Random emails sent whenever you remember are worse than no emails at all. They train your audience to ignore you. Have a content calendar, even a rough one.</li>
  <li><strong>No segmentation.</strong> Sending the same email to your entire list regardless of who they are or what they care about is a missed opportunity. Even basic segmentation, like separating new subscribers from long-time customers, can dramatically improve your results.</li>
  <li><strong>All selling, no value.</strong> If people do not get something useful from your emails, they will unsubscribe. Every email should give the reader a reason to be glad they opened it.</li>
  <li><strong>Ignoring mobile.</strong> Over 60% of emails are opened on mobile devices. If your emails look terrible on a phone, you have lost the majority of your audience before they have read a word. Use a clean, simple template that renders well on any screen size.</li>
  <li><strong>Not testing.</strong> Subject lines, send times, content formats. Test them. Small changes can produce big improvements, and you will not know what works best for your audience until you try.</li>
</ul>

<p>Email marketing for small business is not complicated, but it does require intention. Build your list with care. Send content that is actually worth reading. Set up automations so the system works even when you are busy. And do not overlook <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS marketing</a> as a complement to your email strategy. And pay attention to the numbers so you can keep improving.</p>

<p>The businesses that do this well do not just have a marketing channel. They have a direct line to their customers that no algorithm can take away.</p>

